    {
      "claim": "The query is a 1-cyclopropyl-6-fluoro-8-methoxy-4-oxo-3-carboxylic acid fluoroquinolone; reference fluoroquinolones with this core have measured human absolute oral bioavailability of 87.6–100% (trovafloxacin, levofloxacin, ofloxacin), and antofloxacin shows an oral/IV AUC ratio of about 1.1.",
      "card_ids": ["C04", "C13", "C14", "C23", "C26", "C27", "C28", "C32", "C34", "C11", "C22"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records provide supportive context, not direct F measurements: moxifloxacin shows Cmax 4–6 µg/mL and AUC 40–60 µg·h/mL after oral 400 mg; zabofloxacin shows Cmax ~1.9 µg/mL and AUC ~11.1–11.3 µg·h/mL after oral 400 mg; a related fluoroquinolone shows Cmax 7 mg/L and AUC 76.2 mg·h/L after 800 mg.",
      "card_ids": ["C45", "C46", "C47", "C36", "C40", "C42", "C44", "C39", "C41"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 intestinal absorption and solubility records for fluoroquinolones support adequate absorption: ciprofloxacin showed 71% absorption after oral administration in dogs, high solubility in acidic media (10 mg/mL hydrochloride salt; 3.7 mg/mL FaSSGF for an amorphous salt; complete dissolution at pH 1.2), and >90% in vivo dissolution; gatifloxacin showed 40–60 mg/mL solubility at pH 2–5.",
      "card_ids": ["C55", "C57", "C56", "C49", "C50", "C58", "C53", "C54", "C48", "C52", "C59"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No L4 record provides affirmative evidence for F < 20% in this chemotype; the low-F L2 records are from unrelated molecules or conditions (donepezil in rats, encainide in poor-metabolizer cirrhosis) and do not transfer to the query, and the repaglinide dissolution record is not applicable.",
      "card_ids": ["C31", "C33", "C51"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The physicochemical prior is uncertain and not endpoint proof; zwitterionic fluoroquinolones with low logD can still be well absorbed, so the prior does not affirmatively establish F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C04", "C13", "C14", "C55", "C54"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C48", "C49", "C50", "C52", "C53", "C54", "C55", "C56", "C57", "C58", "C59"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability measurement for the exact query molecule is provided; the decision relies on transfer from closely related fluoroquinolones.",
    "Query-specific permeability, solubility, transporter, and first-pass data are absent; the physicochemical prior is uncertain and not endpoint proof.",
    "The 7-substituent in the query is a fluoroalkene-containing piperidine with a primary amine, which differs from the reference fluoroquinolone substituents; its absorption impact is inferred by structural analogy.",
    "L4 records are mostly ciprofloxacin and gatifloxacin solubility/dissolution data plus a dog fraction-absorbed value, not matched oral-versus-IV absolute bioavailability determinations for the query.",
    "No formulation-, dose-, or species-matched data for the exact query are present."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high. The query is a fluoroquinolone with the same 4-oxo-3-carboxylic acid core and 6-fluoro/8-methoxy/1-cyclopropyl pattern as reference fluoroquinolones with measured human absolute oral bioavailability of 87.6–100%. L4 intestinal absorption and solubility records for ciprofloxacin and gatifloxacin support adequate absorption and dissolution for this chemotype and add no affirmative evidence for F < 20%; the uncertain physicochemical prior does not override the transferable experimental evidence."
}

